Abstract
ASRI (Aerospace Systems Research Institute) is developing a sub-orbital rocket (STeVe) and
an orbital rocket (CLV). In this study, the external airflow around these rockets was
characterised. Characterisation entailed determining the static aerodynamic coefficients for
varying Mach numbers and angles of attack using CFD and semi-empirical methods (RASAero
and Missile Datcom).
To ensure the characterisation was accurate, five separate cases were used to validate the CFD
model and confirm the accuracy and limitations of the semi-empirical methods. In the
validation chapter, both semi-empirical methods and the CFD model were shown to perform
well compared to experimental results. In general, for all flow regimes, excluding outliers, the
CFD and the semi-empirical methods were within 15% of the experimental wind tunnel results.
In most cases, the CFD and the semi-empirical methods were within 5% of the experimental
wind tunnel results.
Using the validated model, various aerodynamic coefficients were determined for both launch
vehicles. Using these aerodynamic characterisation results, the drag force was determined for
STeVe and CLV for a given flight trajectory, comparing the results from CFD and semi
empirical inputs. For STeVe, Missile Datcom predicted a lower peak drag force by 4.8%, while
RASAero predicted a higher peak drag force by 7.5%, when compared to CFD. For CLV,
Missile Datcom and RASAero both predicted a higher peak drag force by 2.7% and 24.2%
respectively, when compared to CFD.
The aerodynamic heating effects on the STeVe nose cone were investigated, in which CFD
results were compared with analytical heat transfer calculations. The CFD and analytical
temperature results differed by less than 1%. Different nose cone tip radii were simulated to
determine the effect on heat transfer, where negligible surface temperature differences were
observed. However, the maximum heat transfer coefficient decreased as nose tip radius
increased. Missile Datcom was used to modify the STeVe and CLV fuselage geometry,
comparing the effects on CdA and drag force. For STeVe, increasing the diameter by 21%
increased the peak drag force by 19%. For CLV, increasing the diameter by 15% increased the
peak drag force by 37%.


