1. Introduction
The introduction presents a brief background on ASRI, along with an overview of the STeVe
and CLV rockets. It also outlines the aims, objectives, and structure of the thesis.
1.1 Background of ASRI
ASRI (Aerospace Systems Research Institute) was founded in 2009 as a research group,
formally ASReG (Aerospace Systems Research Group), and is now a research institute based
within the University of KwaZulu-Natal’s (UKZN) Discipline of Mechanical Engineering. The
institute has three aims (ASRI, 2024),
1) “to become a global centre of excellence in aerospace propulsion research and
development”
2) “to support South Africa’s space engineering economy”
3) “to develop skilled human capital in aerospace propulsion engineering.”
Since its inception in 2009, ASRI has developed a Phoenix series of rockets, which have
achieved multiple successful launches from the Overberg Test Range (OTR). In March 2021,
the Phoenix-1B Mk 2-R achieved an apogee of 17.9 km (ASRI, 2021), setting a new African maximum altitude record for a hybrid rocket. The institute has been successful in developing
rockets, human capital, test facilities, and launch facilities.
ASRI is currently developing two rockets, namely a Commercial Launch Vehicle (CLV) and a
sub-orbital sounding rocket, STeVe (Sub-orbital Test Vehicle). STeVe will test the components
and sub-systems that will later be used in CLV. CLV is an orbital rocket that will carry and
deploy satellites, representing ASRI’s primary objective. On the path to developing the CLV
and STeVe launch vehicles, ASRI is also developing a liquid rocket engine called SAFFIRE
(South AFrican First Integrated Rocket Engine) and a permanent test and launch facility at the
Overberg Test Range (OTR), among other projects.
1.2 STeVe (Sub-orbital Test Vehicle) and CLV (Commercial Launch Vehicle)
STeVe is a single-stage, sub-orbital sounding rocket, powered by a single SAFFIRE liquid
rocket engine. A sounding rocket is a sub-orbital research rocket, usually with the aim of testing
equipment and recording measurements. These rockets will carry a payload (such as satellites,
or scientific instruments), gaining valuable information during launch and in free fall (near the
apogee of flight).
STeVe, shown in Figure 1, aims to enable testing of several systems, including the SAFFIRE
rocket engines, propellant tank and aerostructure technologies, and CLV subsystems. It will
also build OTR’s space launch capacity, and reach space by achieving an apogee above 100 km.
STeVe consists of a Von Kármán nose cone, a cylindrical fuselage, and four hexagonal cross
sectional fins.


