At the start of this study, both STeVe and CLV were still in the design phase and both
geometries were not yet finalised. Notably, for STeVe, the fuselage diameter was yet to be
confirmed. By July 2024, the initial Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD) simulations were
started for both launch vehicles and the respective geometries were used throughout the thesis.
1.3 Motivation of the Study
Obtaining the aerodynamic coefficients of a rocket is important to predict the flight trajectory
and the forces acting on the rocket structure. The aerodynamic coefficients can be accurately
determined using CFD software, which was the main goal of this study. An external CFD
simulation to obtain the aerodynamic coefficients had not been performed on STeVe or CLV
prior to this work.
The aerodynamic coefficients can also be obtained using semi-empirical methods, such as those
employed by Missile Datcom or RASAero. The advantage of semi-empirical methods is that
they have virtually zero computational cost, however, some accuracy is lost when compared to
CFD simulations. A secondary goal of this study was to compare the semi-empirical methods
to empirical and CFD results to validate the accuracy and observe the deficiencies of the semi
empirical methods.
Both STeVe and CLV will travel at close to hypersonic speeds, where aerodynamic heating
effects will cause substantial increases in rocket surface temperatures, especially at the nose
cone. These temperatures must be known and mitigated; therefore, the effects of aerodynamic
heating were investigated here.
Since both the launch vehicles were in the design phase during this thesis, the geometries had
scope to be modified. Both geometries were changed, analysing the effects on drag and heat
transfer.


