2.1.5 Turbulence Modelling
The Navier-Stokes equations describe all fluid flow, including turbulence and instantaneous
velocity fluctuations. However, the equations have no analytical solution due to their
complexity. There are two methods to apply the Navier-Stokes equations in CFD: either solving
them numerically or reducing and simplifying them before solving. This chapter examines these
methods to simulate turbulent flow.
Due to the high Reynolds number flow that occurs in external airflow over rockets, there will
be turbulent flow, which CFD turbulence models aim to capture. Three main approaches are
used to model turbulence: Direct Numerical Simulation (DNS), Large Eddy Simulation (LES),
and Reynolds Averaged Navier-Stokes (RANS).
DNS: It is possible to model turbulent flow by solving the Navier-Stokes equations numerically,
and not rely on turbulence modelling. This is the principle behind DNS, which requires large
computational resources. No information is available regarding the exact computational cost of
DNS, except that the computational cost is orders of magnitude larger than RANS. Therefore,
DNS was not chosen for this study.
LES: LES is a simpler version of DNS, as it separates the flow into large and small-scale
turbulent structures. LES aims to capture the larger turbulent structures, while ignoring the
smaller turbulent structures. This reduces mesh size, since the smaller cells required to model
smaller turbulent structures are ignored. However, solution accuracy is not sacrificed, since
most of the turbulent kinetic energy is contained in the large turbulent structures. Even though
computational resources are saved using this method, LES is still far too computationally
demanding for this study.
RANS: RANS differs from DNS and LES, as it aims to model the entire flow, and not directly
resolve the turbulent structures. In reality, for steady, turbulent flow, the velocity profile in a
specific area at a point in time is never going to be exactly the same as the velocity profile in
that same area at a different point in time. The velocity profile at a point in time is defined as
the instantaneous velocity profile. In other words, the instantaneous velocity profile varies with
time in steady flow, as shown for steady flow over a flat plate at two time points, t1 and t2, in
Figure 9.


