2.1.7 Eddy Viscosity Models
Eddy viscosity models are a class of turbulence model that approximate the eddy viscosity term,
to determine the Reynolds stress terms. There are three broad categories of eddy viscosity
models: Algebraic models, one equation models, and two equation models.
Algebraic Models (Zero Equation Models): Solutions to additional equations are not
required. The eddy viscosity term can be directly calculated using flow variables. These models
are too simple for most flow cases. Two common algebraic models are the ‘ aldwin- omax’ model and the ‘Cebeci-Smith’ model.
One Equation Models: One additional transport equation is introduced. The most common
one equation model is ‘Spalart-Allmaras’, which solves for the turbulent kinematic viscosity,
νt. Spalart Allmaras was developed for the aerospace industry involving wall-bounded and
aerodynamic flows, and can also perform well with adverse pressure gradients. It is
recommended to aim for a y+ of ~1 when using Spalart Allmaras.
Two Equation Models: Two additional transport equations are introduced. The three most
popular two equation models (which have become the industry standard turbulence models) are
the k − ε, k − ω, and the k − ω SST (Shear Stress Transport) models.
k − ε is most accurate in modelling flow in the free stream; however, it is inaccurate when
