2.2 Aerodynamics
Aerodynamics is the study of the interaction of air flowing over bodies. The following chapters
describe important concepts in aerodynamics, relevant to this study.
2.2.1 Drag
Drag is the force that acts parallel and opposite to the relative motion of the fluid around an
object, through the centre of pressure of the object. Three factors affect drag: the object
geometry, the relative velocity of the object in the fluid, and the fluid properties. Multiple kinds
of drag force arise when analysing rocket flight, which are described below. Firstly, the total
drag can be decomposed into skin friction drag and pressure drag.
Skin friction drag: this is a result of the friction between the fluid and the object moving
through the fluid. Due to the differences in velocity between the fluid layers (in the boundary
layer), there are viscous shear forces between the layers, resulting in friction drag.
Pressure drag (form drag): This is caused by the pressure difference between the leading and
trailing sections of an object. There is a stagnation point at the leading edge of an object, where
the dynamic pressure is converted into static pressure, resulting in a high pressure zone at the


18
leading edge. Often there are sharp angles at the trailing section of objects, where the fluid
separates from the object's surface. This creates a wake (recirculation zone) at the trailing
section of the object, resulting in a low static pressure. Due to the pressure difference, the object
is ‘pushed’ in the direction of high-to-low pressure, which opposes the motion of the object, as
shown in Figure 12. Pressure drag can also be decomposed further into wave drag and base
drag.
Figure 12. Pressure Drag
Wave drag: This is a type of pressure drag caused by shock wave formation in transonic and
supersonic flight. Wave drag is a sudden and large increase in drag when shock waves are
present (typically around Mach 0.8), and the onset of wave drag can easily be seen in drag
coefficient vs. Mach number plots. Wave drag is directly proportional the cross-sectional area
of the object (Whitcomb, 1960) and is independent of viscous effects in the fluid. To reduce
wave drag, the object’s cross-sectional area should ideally be as small as possible and have
smooth changes in cross-sectional area along the length of the object.
Base drag: This is a type of pressure drag that is caused by sharp angles at the trailing edges
of objects. When objects have blunt trailing edges, a wake forms behind the trailing face of the
object, creating an area of low static pressure. This pressure difference typically exerts a force
in a direction opposite to that of the object’s travel. Base drag can be reduced by creating a
‘boat tail’ shape at the trailing edge of an object to reduce flow separation.
There are two additional kinds of drag: lift induced drag and interference drag. These kinds of
drag both form part of pressure and skin friction drag; however, neither fits entirely into a single
category.
Lift induced drag: This is a result of lift and occurs at the rocket fins at a non-zero angle of
attack. Due to the angled fins, on one side of the fin there is a high-pressure zone, and a low
pressure zone on the other side of the fin. This creates a flow from the high-pressure side, along
the fin, towards the tip, around the tip, and along the low-pressure side of the fin. This results
in vortices at the fin tip, which forces the fluid near the fin tip from the high-pressure side to
the low-pressure side of the fin. Lift induced drag in rockets is only a concern if the fins are
canted or the rocket is at a non-zero angle of attack.
Interference drag: This is when fluid flowing across one section of the aircraft mixes with
fluid flowing from another section of the aircraft. This occurs when there are junctions between
different geometries, such as the junction of the fins and the rocket body in rockets.
