2.2.2 Lift
Lift acts perpendicular to the relative motion of the fluid around an object, through the centre
of pressure. In rocketry, lift will only occur at non-zero angles of attack.
2.2.3 Angle of Attack
The angle of attack, α, is the difference in the angle between the relative fluid velocity and the
axial line through the rocket body (or chord line). A positive angle of attack is when the aircraft
is pitched up, relative to the fluid velocity, and a negative angle of attack is when the aircraft is
pitched down, relative to the fluid velocity. Figure 13 shows a positive angle of attack for a
rocket.
Figure 13. Positive Angle of Attack
2.2.4 Axial and Normal Force
The drag force acts parallel with the relative motion of the fluid around an object, through the
centre of pressure. In contrast, the axial force acts parallel with the length of the object (or chord
line), through the centre of pressure. The lift force acts perpendicular to the relative motion of
the fluid around an object, through the centre of pressure. In contrast, the normal force acts
perpendicular to the length of the object (or chord line), through the centre of pressure.
At 0° angle of attack, the axial force is equal to the drag force, and the normal force is equal to
the lift force. When the angle of attack changes, the lift, drag, normal, and axial forces are
related by the following set of equations:
FL = FN cos(α) − FA sin(α) 2.33
FD = FN sin(α) + FA cos(α) 2.34
FN = FL cos(α) + FD sin(α) 2.35
FA = −FL sin(α) + FD cos(α) 2.36
where FL, FD, FN, and FA are the lift, drag, normal, and axial force respectively.
