2.2.5 Aerodynamic Coefficients
Aerodynamic coefficients can be categorised into either static or dynamic coefficients. Static
coefficients characterise the aircraft aerodynamics at constant and steady flow properties. These
coefficients can be used for the initial response of the aircraft, or if the aircraft only makes
gradual changes in its orientation during its flight. Dynamic coefficients characterise the aircraft
aerodynamics in unsteady flow conditions and relate to aircraft stability.
If an aircraft is performing a rapid manoeuvre, the next iteration depends not only on the current
flow properties, but also on the flow properties of the previous iteration. This is where dynamic
coefficients, such as the roll/pitch/yaw damping moment coefficients are used. Dynamic
coefficients were outside of the scope of this study, therefore only static coefficients were
analysed.
The drag, lift, normal, and axial force coefficients are non-dimensional, static coefficients used
to characterise aircraft performance and are calculated using the same equation,
Cx = 2 ∙ Fx
