2.2.7 Shock Waves
Qualitative analysis can be very useful when analysing the flow phenomena in high-speed
external aerodynamics. In supersonic flight, the flow phenomenon that is largely responsible
for drag is shock waves, which are unavoidable when travelling at supersonic speeds. A shock
wave can be characterised by a discontinuous and abrupt increase in the Mach number, pressure,
temperature, and density shown in Figure 15.
Figure 15. Shock Wave Flow Properties
Shock waves can be categorised into three types, by the shape formed: Normal, oblique, and
bow shocks, as pictured in Figure 16. Normal shocks propagate perpendicular to the flow
direction. Oblique shocks propagate at some angle (0° < θ < 90°) to the flow direction, and
form at the nose tip, leading edges, or any sharp geometric change on a rocket body. Bow
shocks (detached shocks) form at a distance from the leading edge of an object, due to the large
rounded leading edge of the object, and unlike normal and oblique shocks, bow shocks are
curved.
Figure 16. Types of Shocks
The rocket geometry can be adjusted to allow for the formation of either oblique or bow shocks.
Bow shocks produce greater drag than oblique shocks, and this can be useful when slowing
down an object, such as for capsule re-entry. However, this is extremely detrimental for STeVe
and CLV.
