2.3 Aerodynamic Heating
Aerodynamic heating is when the temperature of the air surrounding an object increases due to
stagnation and viscous effects.
• Stagnation effects: When a fluid is slowed down, the fluid’s kinetic energy is
transferred into thermal energy (heat) into the surrounding fluid.
• Viscous effects: In the boundary layer, the flow increases in velocity as the distance
away from the wall increases. Due to this velocity difference, there is friction between
neighbouring fluid layers, which causes the surrounding fluid to be heated.
The impact of aerodynamic heating becomes more of a problem as the velocity increases,
because stagnation and viscous heating effects both increase with velocity. The areas on a
rocket where aerodynamic heating would be greatest are the parts that first contact the
oncoming flow, such as the nose cone and fins. This is because stagnation heating contributes
more to the maximum temperature than viscous heating.
As a part of this master's study, the heat transfer and surface temperature distribution were
analysed for the nose cone of STeVe. The effects of aerodynamic heating were analysed using
CFD, with the required theory detailed in this chapter.
2.3.1 Thermal Boundary Layer
Previous mention of boundary layer theory in Chapter 2.1.2 only discussed the velocity profile.
However, similarly to the velocity boundary layer, there also exists a thermal boundary layer,
and temperature profile, as shown in Figure 17.
Figure 17. Temperature Profile and Thermal Boundary Layer for a Flat Plate
Unlike the velocity profile, which has a zero velocity at the wall, the temperature is at a
maximum at the wall. For clarification, the temperature at the wall refers to the temperature of
the first cell, nearest the wall, which is different to the wall temperature, which is the
temperature of the solid wall. In reality, there is a temperature gradient across the wall.
However, for this analysis, the temperature across the wall will be considered uniform.
