2.4 Meshing
Creating a mesh is one step, if not the most crucial in the CFD process. CFD functions by
dividing the fluid volume into a finite number of small elements, called a mesh. The mesh will
influence convergence, accuracy, precision, and the speed of the simulation. Due to the
importance of mesh selection, this chapter of the literature review presents and analyses
research on different elements, methods, and software that can be used in the meshing process.
2.4.1 Types of Mesh Elements
The most common three-dimensional meshing elements available are hexahedral, tetrahedral,
and polyhedral elements, as shown in Figure 18 (Sosnowski et al., 2019). In this chapter,
literature examining the effectiveness of the different meshing elements is provided, beginning
with a brief description of the elements:
1) Hexahedral: Elements with 6 quadrilateral faces, 8 vertices, and 12 edges.
2) Tetrahedral: Elements with 4 triangular faces, 4 vertices, and 6 edges.
3) Polyhedral: Elements with more than 4 polygonal faces.
