2.4.2 Difference in Software Approach
Each CFD software approaches meshing differently. In this study, Ansys Fluent meshing and
Star-CCM+ were chosen for comparison, with the optimal meshing software chosen to use for
the rest of the study.
Ansys Fluent meshing uses a proprietary meshing technique called Mosaic meshing. According
to Ansys, Mosaic meshing “accelerates the meshing process with a reduced face count, higher
quality cells and efficient parallel scalability" (Ansys, no date). Mosaic meshing uses
polyhedral elements to connect different element types to each other. Due to the complex nature
of the rocket geometry, with thin fins and a sharp nose cone; a purely hexahedral element type
is not possible. Ansys Fluent meshing can be used to create a poly-hexcore mesh (as shown in
Figure 21), which comprises of three different mesh types:


29
1) Poly-prism elements in the boundary layer, near the object surface.
2) Structured hexahedral elements in the main fluid domain.
3) Polyhedral elements connecting the prism layer elements to the main fluid domain.
Figure 21. Ansys Poly-hexcore mesh (Ansys, 2020)
Ansys Fluent meshing has four element options for meshing the volume. These options are
explained below as background to the following sections:
1) Polyhedral: The entire fluid domain is filled with polyhedral elements.
2) Poly-hexcore: The bulk of the fluid domain is filled with high-quality hexahedral
elements with poly-prism elements in the boundary layer. In the transition regions
between the boundary layer and the bulk mesh, polyhedral elements are used.
3) Hexcore: The entire fluid domain is filled with hexahedral elements. Often this is not
possible due to complex geometry, so tetrahedral elements are used in combination
with the hexahedral elements.
4) Tetrahedral: The entire fluid domain is filled with tetrahedral elements.
Zore et al. (2019) analysed the effectiveness of a poly-hexcore mesh, compared to a hexcore
mesh, for a 100-passenger jet airliner. It was found that (in general) using a poly-hexcore mesh
reduces the total element count by 20% to 50%, when compared to a hexcore mesh. This in turn
speeds up the Fluent solver by 10% to 50%. When comparing poly-hex with hexcore in terms
of the jet airliner: the total element count was reduced by 48%, and the computational time was
reduced by 41%. For the lift coefficient at an 18.58° angle of attack, the poly-hexcore and
hexcore mesh were, respectively, ~1.5% and ~0.4% higher than wind tunnel data, as shown in
Figure 22. This confirms the accuracy of both meshes, with the hexcore mesh being slightly
closer to the experimental results, than the poly-hexcore mesh.
