The other CFD software option was Star-CCM+, which has three meshing options for the
volume mesh:
1) Trimmed cell: A trimmed cell mesh is a structured mesh that uses entirely hexahedral
elements in the volume. However, in some cases with complex geometries, in the
transition between the prism layers and the volume, some tetrahedral and polyhedral
elements are generated. Trimmed cell meshes are fast and efficient to generate.
2) Polyhedral: An unstructured mesh where only polyhedral elements are used. Polyhedral
meshes are suitable for complex geometries.
3) Tetrahedral: An unstructured mesh where only tetrahedral elements are used.
Each of these volume mesh options can be combined with one of the following prism layer
meshing options:
1) Prism layer mesher: This generates a volume around the surface based on the total
specified prism layer thickness. Each prism layer is then generated until that volume is
filled.
2) Advancing layer mesher: This generates each entire layer successively, starting with
the prism layer closest to the surface. Each prism layer is generated until the total
number of prism layers specified is reached.
Both the prism layer meshing options seem similar; however, there are some key differences.
The advancing layer mesher keeps the total prism layer thickness and the first layer thickness
constant over the entire model geometry. This can result in some poor prism cells over curves,
as there might be a large volume change between neighbouring cells in the prism layer. The
advancing layer mesher also takes a substantially longer time to generate a mesh, compared to
the prism layer mesher. In contrast, the prism layer mesher has many input parameters that can
be used to adapt sections of the prism layer to capture complex features in the model. The prism
layers can be adapted to fit certain geometric features or be removed entirely for areas where
prism layers are not required, which is not possible when using the advancing layer mesher.
Star-CCM+ and Ansys Fluent both use different approaches to meshing. As discussed above,
there is positive literature regarding the accuracy and efficiency of Ansys poly-hexcore meshes.
However, due to the limited literature available comparing Star-CCM+ and Ansys Fluent
meshing, it was decided to use the first validation case to compare the two meshing options.
The optimal meshing option was then used for the rest of the study.
