2.5 Semi-Empirical Aeroprediction Codes
Semi-empirical aeroprediction codes determine the aerodynamic coefficients of rocket
geometries using a combination of analytical calculations and experimental data. Two semi
empirical codes, RASAero and Missile Datcom, were used in this research project as another
data source to compare with the CFD model and to investigate the accuracy of the semi
empirical codes.
Engineers at ASRI use RASAero and Missile Datcom to determine the aerodynamic
coefficients of rockets being developed, within an in-house six-degree-of-freedom code called
PyROPS. The coefficients are supplied to the in-house software to determine the rocket flight
path, apogee, and impact points. ASRI has not validated the aerodynamic coefficients from the
semi-empirical methods against CFD data. It would be useful to know the accuracy of various
aerodynamic coefficients at different Mach numbers and angle of attacks for future use.
2.5.1 RASAero
RASAero (Rogers AeroScience Aero) is a free software application that can perform
aerodynamic analyses and flight simulations for model rockets to sounding rockets. It uses
empirical wind tunnel and free flight data combined with analytical calculations to determine
the aerodynamic coefficients. A large database of interference effects between different bodies
is used to obtain results for different geometry combinations. RASAero has been calibrated
against “NACA and NASA wind tunnel model data, free-flight model data and sounding rocket
data, missile aerodynamic data, and professional engineering method missile aerodynamic
analysis programs, against which in comparison tests RASAero has demonstrated equivalent
numerical accuracy” (Rogers and Cooper, 2019b). The latest RASAero software (version
1.0.2.0) was used in this study.


