3. Model Validation
When designing any aerial vehicle, it is important to predict the aerodynamic performance
under varying conditions, such as Mach number, altitude, and angle of attack. To achieve this,
the aerodynamic characteristics must be determined. Aerodynamic characteristics refer to the
properties of an aircraft that influence the surrounding air flow. These characteristics can be
determined experimentally (wind tunnel testing), using CFD simulations, semi-empirical
aeroprediction codes, or analytical calculations. CFD simulations are a relatively inexpensive
and quick method to obtain these aerodynamic coefficients. However, the results from the CFD
simulations must be validated to ensure accurate and reliable results.
Chapter 3.1 explains fully the process of validation, and how it differs from verification. The
rest of this chapter then addresses the entire validation process done in this study, from defining
the geometry to creating the flow models, and finally discusses the results.
3.1 Validation and Verification
Since validation is a fundamental part of this study, this chapter clarifies the subject of
validation and how it differs from verification.
When discussing validation and verification, the concepts of error, accuracy, and precision must
first be mentioned. Error is defined as “a recognisable deficiency in any phase or activity of
modelling and simulation that is not due to lack of knowledge.” (AIAA G-077-1998). Accuracy
and precision are two measures used to observe and analyse errors in results. Accuracy is a
measure of the difference between the obtained results and the actual results, while precision is
a measure of the difference between just the obtained results. Since semi-empirical methods
and most CFD codes are deterministic in nature, precision is not of importance in this study.
However, measuring accuracy must be discussed further.
Validation and verification are both parameters used to assess the accuracy of a solution.
erification is “The process of determining that a model implementation accurately represents
the developer's conceptual description of the model and the solution to the model.” (AIAA G
077-1998). Since a CFD code is not being created or modified in this study, verification was
not required. alidation is “the process of determining the degree to which a model is an
accurate representation of the real world from the perspective of the intended uses of the
model.” (AIAA G-077-1998). Validation compares results with experimental data, and in the
case of this study, CFD results were validated by comparing them with experimental wind
tunnel data.
To confirm that the results from the CFD model are accurate to reality, the results must be
validated against experimental data from wind tunnel testing as wind tunnels capture flow
phenomena in reality. Comparing against other CFD simulations, semi-empirical
aeroprediction codes, or analytical calculations is also important and was done in this study;
however, technically speaking, this would not be considered validation.
In this study, five different validation cases were used to test various parameters that would
affect the accuracy of the CFD model. Some of the parameters were the domain size, meshing
techniques, mesh independence, flow models, geometry complexity, different CFD
applications, and the accuracy of various semi-empirical methods, among other options. The


40
different effects of these parameters can then be observed, and this information can be used to
validate the CFD models for the STeVe and CLV rockets.
