3.5 Conclusion
In summary, validation began by researching different rocket geometries that had wind tunnel
data available. Five different validation cases were used, of varying geometry complexities: the
spinner, finner, modified finner, and Maxus rocket.
The Dunn spinner case was used to compare the differences between Star-CCM+ and Ansys
Fluent. It was decided to use Star-CCM+ for the rest of the study due to the better workflow
with JavaScript macros and slightly more accurate peak drag prediction.
A mesh independence and domain independence study were conducted at Mach 0.6 for the
Dunn Spinner validation. The number of elements it took to achieve convergence was larger
than expected. It was confirmed that the domain size chosen was already in the converged
region.
For low angles of attack, the drag and normal force coefficients were predicted accurately for
all validation cases. The only substantial inaccuracies occurred when simulating at large angles
of attack (>15°) at high Mach numbers (>3.86). However, these large angles of attack and Mach
number combinations are unrealistic for real rocket flight.
Missile Datcom and RASAero were close to experimental data in terms of predicting
aerodynamic coefficients. Both methods were more accurate in the transonic and supersonic
regions, than the subsonic region. RASAero consistently underpredicted drag in the subsonic
region. A noticeable problem with Missile Datcom is that there are often significant unrealistic
jumps in values between adjacent Mach numbers, as observed in the Dunn Spinner and Dunn
Finner validation cases. This is likely due to the code changing correlations from the transonic
to supersonic region. In the Missile Datcom user’s manual (Air Force Research Laboratory,


58
1998) it is stated that the body alone aerodynamic methodology switches from
subsonic/transonic correlations to supersonic correlations at Mach 1.2. The fin alone
aerodynamic methodology switches from transonic correlations to supersonic correlations at
Mach 1.4. It therefore makes sense, that in some instances there might be substantial changes
around this transition point between correlations. Missile Datcom also overpredicted the drag
for the Maxus rocket by a substantial amount.
In summary, the CFD model created in Star-CCM+ was close to experimental data for existing
rocket geometries. This was true for varying geometry complexities, Mach numbers, and angles
of attack, when measuring the drag and normal force coefficients. Both semi-empirical methods
(RASAero and Missile Datcom) were accurate, besides for occasional flaws in the Missile
Datcom results. The same methods used to create the validation simulations were used for the
STeVe and CLV simulations, to maintain accuracy.


