4.3 Meshing
When dealing with high-speed external flow over rockets, meshing can be broken down into
three areas of importance: The boundary layer, the wake, and the free stream, as shown in
Figure 55. Each of these areas contributes differently to solution accuracy and computational
time; therefore, each area is meshed differently to optimise performance.
Figure 55. Meshing Areas
Boundary layer: The meshing technique used in the boundary layer is to create small,
structured cells (prism layers or inflation layers) near the rocket surface to adequately capture
the boundary layer formation. The process of correctly sizing the boundary layer is described
in Chapter 2.1.4. A y+ of 1 was targeted to adequately resolve the boundary layer without the
use of non-linear wall functions. ‘Sizing the boundary layer’ involved determining the first cell
height, the boundary layer thickness, the total prism layer thickness, and the growth rate. In the
boundary layer, there are some areas where the prism layers need to be small, and some areas
where prism layers do not need to be small, or might not be required at all. In the direction of
the flow, the cells can be large; and perpendicular to the direction of flow, the cells must be
small. This is because changes in flow properties are greater perpendicular to the direction of
flow in the boundary layer. On the fuselage, small prism layers are crucial. However, in the
areas of stagnation and recirculation (nose cone tip, fin leading and trailing edges, rocket base)
there is not the same boundary layer profile that is seen on the fuselage body. Therefore,
structured prism layers are not as important in the areas of stagnation and recirculation. In these
areas, structured prism layers often create poor quality cells due to the sharp rocket geometry.
By removing the prism layers in those areas, the main cause of many meshing and convergence
problems are also removed, and small polyhedral cells can be used instead.
Wake: The wake resolution has a significant impact on the upstream flow. To capture the wake
effectively, the wake refinement was split into three regions: The plume, wake 1, and wake 2,
as shown in Figure 56. The plume aimed to just capture the plume and recirculation at the base
of the rocket, and was the finest region. Wake 1 captured the wake from the plume to roughly
halfway to the domain outlet, and was coarser. Wake 2 captured the wake to the domain outlet,
and was even coarser.
