6. Aerodynamic Heating
This chapter addresses the process and results of the aerodynamic heating analysis for this
study. The important theory regarding aerodynamic heating is found in Chapter 2.3.
6.1 Introduction
In this study, the heat transfer coefficient and surface temperature were analysed for the nose
cone of STeVe. As part of previous work done at ASRI, Sabbagha (2024) analytically
determined the heat transfer coefficient and temperature along STe e’s nose cone. He
approximated the nose cone as a flat plate parallel to the oncoming flow, where forced
convection heat transfer dominated. For the nose cone tip temperature, equations from Jerger
(1960) were used. Sabbagha (2024) concluded that with a pointed nose cone, virtually no
material could withstand the temperatures at the nose cone tip. Therefore, further analysis was
required on using rounded nose cone tips to reduce the temperature. The aerodynamic heating
analysis in this study had the following aims:
1) Compare the analytical heat transfer coefficient and surface temperature results with
CFD results.
2) Determine the heat transfer coefficient and surface temperature for varying nose cone
tip radii.
6.2 CFD Setup
An identical setup to the other STeVe simulations was used, with the process described in
Chapter 4. The only change was inputting the thermal conductivity, k, to a value of
2.5695 W/m.K using Sutherland’s aw.
Sutherland’s aw can be used to define the thermal conductivity of pure and non-reacting gases
as a function of temperature.
